WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.681
Begin by framing Lesson Title: Foundations of
Cybersecurity Principles as a question about

2
00:00:08.681 --> 00:00:12.000
inputs, decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:20.053
It will cover cybersecurity principles,
policies and mechanisms; core knowledge areas

4
00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:27.374
of data, software, component, connection,
system, human, organizational and societal

5
00:00:27.374 --> 00:00:33.231
security; and crosscutting concepts of
confidentiality, integrity, availability,

6
00:00:33.231 --> 00:00:37.624
risk, adversarial thinking, and systems
thinking.

7
00:00:37.624 --> 00:00:44.506
Consider this worked case: A hospital's
electronic health records must remain

8
00:00:44.506 --> 00:00:50.761
confidential from unauthorized users, maintain
integrity against tampering, and remain

9
00:00:50.761 --> 00:00:54.515
available during peak patient intake hours.

10
00:00:54.515 --> 00:01:01.530
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

11
00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.031
conclusion.

12
00:01:02.031 --> 00:01:07.968
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

13
00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:14.458
Now define Lesson Title: Cryptographic
Standards and Confidentiality precisely enough

14
00:01:14.458 --> 00:01:18.915
that another learner could test the
definition.

15
00:01:18.915 --> 00:01:25.227
Encryption supports confidentiality when keys
and modes are used correctly.

16
00:01:25.227 --> 00:01:34.789
Consider this worked case: AES-256 encryption
with proper key management ensures patient

17
00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:41.164
data remains unreadable to unauthorized
personnel, satisfying confidentiality

18
00:01:41.164 --> 00:01:41.961
requirements.

19
00:01:41.961 --> 00:01:48.493
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

20
00:01:48.493 --> 00:01:50.868
evidence would distinguish them.

21
00:01:50.868 --> 00:01:57.102
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

22
00:01:57.102 --> 00:02:04.470
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

23
00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:06.680
throughout the analysis.

24
00:02:06.794 --> 00:02:14.458
With the foundation in place, examine how
Lesson Title: Integrity and Authentication

25
00:02:14.458 --> 00:02:18.290
Mechanisms operates in a concrete sequence.

26
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:25.504
Message authentication and digital signatures
address integrity and authenticity in

27
00:02:25.504 --> 00:02:26.947
different ways.

28
00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:33.991
Consider this worked case: A digital signature
on a software update verifies the developer's

29
00:02:33.991 --> 00:02:39.525
identity and confirms the code has not been
modified during transmission.

30
00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:44.478
Digital signature (integrity + authenticity).

31
00:02:44.478 --> 00:02:51.712
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

32
00:02:51.712 --> 00:02:57.525
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

33
00:02:57.626 --> 00:03:04.867
Next, compare the choices involved in Lesson
Title: Secure Software Development Framework

34
00:03:04.867 --> 00:03:09.091
(SSDF) and make the decision criteria
explicit.

35
00:03:09.091 --> 00:03:15.716
The Secure Software Development Framework
groups practices into four practice groups.

36
00:03:15.716 --> 00:03:23.536
Consider this worked case: A development team
uses the 'Prepare the Organization' group to

37
00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:29.122
establish secure coding standards and training
before writing any code.

38
00:03:29.122 --> 00:03:36.301
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

39
00:03:36.301 --> 00:03:37.497
the goalposts.

40
00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:44.529
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

41
00:03:44.642 --> 00:03:51.952
Apply Lesson Title: Security Requirements and
Verification to a worked situation instead of

42
00:03:51.952 --> 00:03:55.326
treating it as an isolated definition.

43
00:03:55.326 --> 00:04:02.638
Verification provides evidence that selected
practices and controls were performed.

44
00:04:02.638 --> 00:04:10.367
Consider this worked case: A compliance
officer reviews a checklist to verify that all

45
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:15.888
encryption standards and access controls were
implemented before software release.

46
00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:23.710
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

47
00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:24.232
step.

48
00:04:24.232 --> 00:04:29.373
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

49
00:04:29.498 --> 00:04:35.434
Examine the tradeoffs in Lesson Title:
Vulnerability Management and Response,

50
00:04:35.434 --> 00:04:40.182
including what improves and what becomes more
difficult.

51
00:04:40.182 --> 00:04:46.970
Respond to Vulnerabilities identifies,
analyzes, remediates, and learns from

52
00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.479
discovered weaknesses.

53
00:04:48.479 --> 00:04:55.562
Consider this worked case: After a penetration
test reveals an unpatched database

54
00:04:55.562 --> 00:05:02.055
vulnerability, the team patches the code,
updates documentation, and trains developers

55
00:05:02.055 --> 00:05:04.416
on secure input validation.

56
00:05:04.416 --> 00:05:11.447
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

57
00:05:11.447 --> 00:05:18.947
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

58
00:05:19.058 --> 00:05:26.087
Stress-test the reasoning around Lesson Title:
Adversarial Thinking and Threat Modeling with

59
00:05:26.087 --> 00:05:31.945
an edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

60
00:05:31.945 --> 00:05:40.339
Security reasoning identifies assets, trust
boundaries, threats, controls, evidence,

61
00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:44.070
residual risk, and responsibilities.

62
00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:51.775
Consider this worked case: An analyst maps a
web application's trust boundary, identifies a

63
00:05:51.775 --> 00:05:58.929
potential SQL injection threat, and assigns a
control to validate all user inputs.

64
00:05:58.929 --> 00:06:05.523
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

65
00:06:05.523 --> 00:06:12.320
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

66
00:06:12.434 --> 00:06:20.471
Use Lesson Title: Systems Thinking and
Crosscutting Concepts to diagnose a failure by

67
00:06:20.471 --> 00:06:24.180
separating symptoms, causes, and corrective
action.

68
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:31.199
This course provides a broad introduction to
cybersecurity principles and practices, and

69
00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:37.634
emphasizes policies and mechanisms for
building secure and trusted computer systems.

70
00:06:37.634 --> 00:06:45.643
Consider this worked case: A policy requiring
multi-factor authentication reduces risk by

71
00:06:45.643 --> 00:06:51.649
addressing both human behavior and system
access points simultaneously.

72
00:06:51.649 --> 00:06:59.537
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

73
00:06:59.537 --> 00:07:01.227
smallest plausible cause.

74
00:07:01.227 --> 00:07:08.727
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

75
00:07:08.834 --> 00:07:15.728
Connect Lesson Title: Privacy, Ethics, and
Legal Compliance to the earlier ideas and

76
00:07:15.728 --> 00:07:19.971
explain how the pieces work as one system.

77
00:07:19.971 --> 00:07:26.299
Topics in privacy, and legal and ethical
aspects will also be emphasized.

78
00:07:26.299 --> 00:07:33.674
Consider this worked case: A healthcare app
implements data minimization and consent

79
00:07:33.674 --> 00:07:39.205
mechanisms to comply with privacy regulations
while maintaining security.

80
00:07:39.205 --> 00:07:48.661
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

81
00:07:48.661 --> 00:07:50.237
across representations.

82
00:07:50.237 --> 00:07:57.908
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

83
00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:04.723
Finish with Lesson Title: Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

84
00:08:04.723 --> 00:08:06.788
guide a new analysis.

85
00:08:06.788 --> 00:08:13.070
This course requires the knowledge of computer
science theory and concepts of computer

86
00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:13.553
systems.

87
00:08:13.553 --> 00:08:21.577
Consider this worked case: An auditor reviews
a cloud migration plan against the checklist

88
00:08:21.577 --> 00:08:27.881
to verify encryption, access controls, backup
availability, and risk mitigation steps.

89
00:08:27.881 --> 00:08:36.020
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

90
00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.272
verification step.

91
00:08:37.272 --> 00:08:44.068
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

92
00:08:44.068 --> 00:08:44.553
intuition.
